     The term <dfn>NFC content</dfn> denotes all bytes sent to or received from
     an <a>NFC tag</a>. In the current API it is synonym to <a>NDEF message</a>.
   </p>
+  <p>
+    <dfn>ISO-DEP</dfn> is the half-duplex block transmission protocol defined
+    in [[!ISO14443-4]], used by contactless smart cards on top of NFC-A or
+    NFC-B.
+  </p>
+  <p>
+    A <dfn>command APDU</dfn> is an application protocol data unit sent to a
+    smart card, consisting of a class byte (<dfn>CLA</dfn>), an instruction
+    byte (<dfn>INS</dfn>), two parameter bytes (<dfn>P1</dfn> and
+    <dfn>P2</dfn>), and optional command data and expected response length,
+    as defined in [[!ISO7816-4]]. A <dfn>response APDU</dfn> is the card's
+    answer, ending with two status bytes. The status bytes `90 00` indicate
+    success.
+  </p>
+  <p>
+    An <dfn>application identifier</dfn> (AID) identifies an application on a
+    smart card, and is used to select it with the <code>SELECT</code> command
+    ([[!ISO7816-4]]).
+  </p>
+  <p>
+    An <dfn data-lt="electronic machine readable travel documents|eMRTD">electronic
+    machine readable travel document</dfn> (eMRTD) is a passport, identity card
+    or other travel document containing a contactless chip that follows
+    [[!ICAO-9303]]. Access to the chip's data requires an access key derived
+    from the document's machine readable zone (MRZ) or card access number
+    (CAN), using Basic Access Control (BAC) or Password Authenticated
+    Connection Establishment (PACE).
+  </p>
+  <p>
+    The <dfn>eMRTD application identifier</dfn> is the byte sequence
+    `A0 00 00 02 47 10 01`.
+  </p>
 </section> <!-- Terminology -->
 
 
