+    <section> <h4>Smart card skimming and relay over ISO-DEP</h4>
+      <dl>
+        <dt><strong>Threat description</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          A malicious web page uses {{NFCIsoDepReader}} to exchange commands
+          with a payment card, transit card or access badge near the device,
+          to read card data or to relay a transaction to a remote terminal.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Affected assets</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          Payment and access credentials, user funds.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Actors</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          Malicious web page owner using Web NFC.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Mitigation, comments</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          The UA only exposes cards that match an <a>application policy</a>,
+          by running the policy's <a>classification algorithm</a> before the
+          page has access to the card. A payment card does not match the
+          <a>eMRTD application policy</a>. The <a>command filter</a> further
+          refuses selection of any application other than the <a>eMRTD
+          application identifier</a> and all proprietary command classes.
+          {{NFCIsoDepReader/scan()}} requires <a>transient activation</a> and
+          permission, and NFC is <a>suspended</a> when the page is not
+          visible.
+        </dd>
+      </dl>
+    </section>
+
+    <section> <h4>Identity document data disclosure</h4>
+      <dl>
+        <dt><strong>Threat description</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          A malicious web page reads the personal data and facial image stored
+          on an <a>eMRTD</a> without the user's informed consent.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Affected assets</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          User identity, biometric data.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Actors</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          Malicious web page owner using Web NFC.
+        </dd>
+        <dt><strong>Mitigation, comments</strong></dt>
+        <dd>
+          An <a>eMRTD</a> discloses no personal data until the reader proves
+          knowledge of the document's access key (BAC or PACE). A page that
+          does not know the key obtains nothing from the chip; a page that has
+          the key, typically because the user typed or photographed the MRZ,
+          has already been given the printed data. The user also has to physically
+          hold the document to the device. The permission prompt SHOULD name
+          the document type. Active authentication and chip authentication
+          produce signatures proving that the chip was present; UAs SHOULD
+          make users aware that the site receives such proof.
+        </dd>
+      </dl>
+    </section>
   </section>
 
